The flag of Tonga consists of a solid red field, a white canton in the upper-left corner, and a red Greek cross centered inside the canton. Consequently, after mapping red pixels to the scalar \(a\) and white pixels to the scalar \(b\), every row of the resulting matrix belongs to one of two families:

- rows lying entirely outside the canton height, or lying inside the canton height but intersecting the horizontal arm of the cross: every entry equals \(a\);
- rows lying inside the canton height but missing the horizontal arm: the leftmost block (the canton width) consists of a central interval of \(a\)’s (the vertical arm) flanked by \(b\)’s, while the remainder of the row equals \(a\).

Thus the matrix possesses at most two distinct row vectors, denoted \(\mathbf{1}\) (the constant row) and \(\mathbf{p}\) (the patterned row). Provided \(a\neq b\), these two vectors are linearly independent: \(\mathbf{1}\) is constant while \(\mathbf{p}\) is not. The row space is therefore exactly two-dimensional, and the rank of the matrix equals 2. This value is attained for any choice \(a\neq b\) and is consequently the maximal possible rank.

