The decomposition \(M=(a-b)R+bJ\) remains the cleanest starting point. The indicator \(R\) is assembled from a small number of combinatorial rectangles (the full red field, minus the white canton, plus the two arms of the cross). Because every horizontal boundary is axis-aligned, the rows of \(R\) fall into at most three distinct patterns: the constant-1 field rows below the canton, the rows through the crossbar (still constant 1 inside the canton width), and the rows that clip only the vertical arm (a single central interval of 1’s flanked by 0’s).  

These three vectors are linearly independent for generic widths—the differences between them have disjoint supports—so \(\operatorname{rank}(R)=3\). Adding the rank-1 all-ones update therefore yields \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le4\), but the extra dimension is already absorbed by the constant field rows, leaving the column space exactly three-dimensional. Attempts to split the lower canton band into two further independent rows fail because those bands differ from the crossbar band only by a scalar multiple on the same support; hence they do not increase the dimension beyond 3. For any sufficiently fine discretization the maximal attainable rank is therefore 3.

