The decomposition \(M=(a-b)R+bJ\) with \(R\) the red-indicator matrix continues to give the sharpest bound. The Tonga geometry yields precisely three distinct row-support patterns for \(R\): the full red field outside the canton, the canton rows intersecting only the vertical arm, and the canton rows intersecting the horizontal arm. These three indicator vectors are linearly independent—their differences have pairwise disjoint supports on the canton columns—so \(\operatorname{rank}(R)=3\). The rank-1 all-ones update is already absorbed into the constant-field rows and does not increase the dimension, leaving \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=3\) for generic \(a\neq b\) and any grid that resolves the cross.  

Claims of rank 2 (advanced by colleagues 1–3) under-count the vertical arm’s distinct support inside the canton and therefore collapse two genuinely independent patterns into one. Arguments that would reach rank 4 require an extra independent horizontal band inside the canton that the flag’s geometry simply does not contain; any such band differs from an existing pattern only by a scalar factor on the same columns. Hence the maximal attainable rank remains exactly 3.

