3.text:
The Tonga flag geometry produces only two distinct row vectors in any discretization: the constant vector of all \(a\)’s (bottom rows wholly outside the canton, plus every row that intersects the horizontal arm of the cross, since that arm spans the full canton width and therefore merges with the surrounding red field) and the single patterned row that intersects only the vertical arm (a narrow central block of \(a\)’s inside the canton, flanked by \(b\)’s inside the canton and then \(a\)’s outside the canton).  

These two vectors are linearly independent whenever \(a\neq b\). No third independent row pattern exists; any finer banding inside the canton is a scalar multiple of one of the two patterns already identified. Consequently the row space is exactly two-dimensional and the maximal attainable rank of the matrix is 2.  

