5.text:
The Tonga flag has a solid red field (value \(a\)) outside a white canton containing a centered, couped red Greek cross (also value \(a\)). The cross consists of a vertical bar of a given thickness together with a horizontal bar whose width strictly exceeds that thickness.  

Consequently any row discretization that resolves the canton yields exactly three distinct row-support patterns in the indicator matrix \(R\):  
- exterior rows lying wholly outside the canton height (constant \(a\));  
- rows intersecting only the vertical bar (narrow central block of \(a\)’s inside the canton);  
- rows intersecting the horizontal bar (wider central block of \(a\)’s inside the canton).  

The three corresponding indicator vectors remain linearly independent because their pairwise differences occupy disjoint column blocks inside the canton; the rank-1 all-ones term contributed by the constant \(bJ\) factor is already spanned by the constant-\(a\) rows and cannot enlarge the column space. No fourth independent pattern exists, and any finer horizontal slice inside the canton is a scalar multiple of one of the three patterns already enumerated.  

Thus \(M=(a-b)R+bJ\) attains rank exactly 3 for generic \(a\neq b\) on every discretization that separates the two arm widths. The rank-2 reduction advanced by colleagues 3 and 5 collapses the two arm patterns, which is impossible once the distinct horizontal extents are respected.  

